 "best_idea": "Decide a fight stance by comparing the estimated chance to win the exchange with a fair share of it (1 over foes plus one), press when clearly ahead, hold when it covers the cost, back off otherwise, and scale all of it by per-creature temperament multipliers near 1.0.",
 "decision": "adapt",
 "decision_why": "The stance rule (press/hold/wait/back-off against many foes with a fair-share reference) fits many-on-one fights, but the donor's equity is a Monte Carlo sample too costly per server tick, so Frankendom needs a cheap estimate and re-tuned thresholds, and temperament must live in catalogue data.",
 "decision_status": "proposed",
 "acceptance": "A player sees a creature hold back when it is outnumbered and only press when clearly ahead; a server test asserts the same fight state gives the same stance on replay from the seeded RNG, and that a creature with a higher aggression value presses more often over a fixed set of states."
